winewayland: ignore a maximized state the compositor gives a fixed-size dialog

Hyprland sets the maximized state on every toplevel when it is mapped, so that
clients draw no decorations (XDGShell.cpp: "this forces apps to not draw CSD"),
and adds all four tiled states when the toplevel is created. A floating dialog
receives both. The driver mirrors the maximized state into WS_MAXIMIZE and
clips the surface to the configured size.
Power BI Desktop shows its legacy HTML dialogs (the unexpected error report,
Data source settings and others of the same family) as a borderless owned form
that starts at 2 x 2 pixels and is resized once the page has measured itself.
With WS_MAXIMIZE set, WinForms treats the form as maximized and only records
the requested size: no SetWindowPos follows. The dialog stayed a 2 x 2 window
without visible content while its owner was disabled, which looked like a
frozen application with nothing to dismiss.
An owned window without a sizing frame is announced to the compositor with
equal minimum and maximum sizes, so the compositor cannot have maximized it.
Drop the state for such a window unless the window itself is maximized.
Windows with a sizing frame and windows without an owner are unchanged.
The style and owner are read before the window data lock is taken: win32u
calls the driver with its own lock held, and the reverse order deadlocked
during startup in a first version of this change.
Applies after wine-11.18-wayland-normal-resize.patch and
wine-11.18-wayland-parent.patch. Wine master had the same code on 2026-11-18.
Wine-derived change: LGPL-2.1-or-later.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -542,6 +542,13 @@
     BOOL restoring_from_minimize = FALSE;
     struct wayland_win_data *data;
     RECT rect, surface_rect;
+    BOOL fixed_dialog;
+
+    /* Read the window before taking the window data lock: win32u calls the
+     * driver with its own lock held, so the order must not be reversed. */
+    style = NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_STYLE);
+    fixed_dialog = !(style & (WS_THICKFRAME | WS_MAXIMIZE)) &&
+                   NtUserGetWindowRelative(hwnd, GW_OWNER);
 
     if (!(data = wayland_win_data_get(hwnd))) return;
     if (!(surface = data->wayland_surface))
@@ -567,6 +574,23 @@
     surface->processing = surface->requested;
     memset(&surface->requested, 0, sizeof(surface->requested));
 
+    /* A compositor may report the maximized state for a window it did not
+     * maximize: Hyprland sets it on every mapped toplevel so that clients
+     * draw no decorations, a floating dialog of fixed size included. Mirrored
+     * into WS_MAXIMIZE, it makes an application ignore its own later resize
+     * (a WinForms form only records the new size while it is maximized), and
+     * the surface is then clipped to the size it had when it was mapped.
+     * An owned window without a sizing frame is announced to the compositor
+     * with equal minimum and maximum sizes and cannot be maximized by it, so
+     * keep the state only if the window itself is maximized. */
+    if (fixed_dialog &&
+        (surface->processing.state & WAYLAND_SURFACE_CONFIG_STATE_MAXIMIZED) &&
+        !(surface->window.state & WAYLAND_SURFACE_CONFIG_STATE_MAXIMIZED))
+    {
+        TRACE("hwnd=%p ignoring maximized state for a fixed-size dialog\n", hwnd);
+        surface->processing.state &= ~WAYLAND_SURFACE_CONFIG_STATE_MAXIMIZED;
+    }
+
     state = surface->processing.state;
     /* A window manager may resize a normal window without marking it tiled
      * or interactively resizing (e.g. PaperWM move_resize_frame). Once the
